Yes. English is actually perfect for that because it already carries both bloodlines: a hard Germanic skeleton and a vast Romance wardrobe. We can let the short Anglo-Saxon words carry force—stand, cut, hold, home, blood, fire—and let the Latinate/French register carry color, nuance, cadence, and atmosphere—luminous, ardor, devotion, elegance, reverberation. The trick is not to perfume every sentence. It is contrast: stone beside velvet, drum beside violin.

Your red-clad pimp and bright bird are versions of the same broader problem: signaling has an audience, a cost, and a radius. The red does not prove power; it advertises a claim about status to a particular reference group. Likewise, brilliant plumage can increase mating success while increasing visibility to predators. Evolution does not seek “maximum beauty” or “maximum safety.” It retains whatever mixture produces enough reproductive success under local conditions.

So you can describe a signal with something like:

S=(\text{amplitude},\text{cost},\text{audience},\text{radius},\text{risk},\text{return})

A peacock’s tail has enormous amplitude and mating return, but also metabolic and predation costs. Camouflage moves the sliders differently. Some organisms even switch: blaze color during courtship, disappear afterward.

That gives you a lovely general operator:

\boxed{\text{Reveal enough to attract; conceal enough to endure.}}

Not only biologically. People do it with clothes, speech, credentials, humor, architecture, brands, ritual, even silence.

And your scale point matters. A signal optimized for one neighborhood can become ridiculous, invisible, or dangerous at another scale. The local king in scarlet may be broadcasting perfectly to twenty people and meaning essentially nothing to twenty million.

So I’d add Projection Radius to the Pantheon:

\text{Who is this signal actually designed to reach?}

That question prevents us from confusing intensity with universality.

As for balancing all of this “for everyone”: I would not try to choose everybody’s equilibrium for them. Better to give them discernment, feedback, and safe degrees of freedom.

\text{agency}
+
\text{guide rails}
+
\text{consequences}
+
\text{feedback}
\rightarrow
\text{self-calibration}

In a Christian frame, faith or the Holy Spirit can serve as the orienting reference—the thing returned to when local signals become noisy. But even there, different people still have to discern, act, learn, repent, revise, and choose. Guidance need not mean puppetry.

And yes, compression and decompression work together. Compression gives you a handle:

\text{vast experience}\rightarrow\text{symbol}

Then you open it again:

\text{symbol}\rightarrow\text{meaning space}

and perhaps recompress it differently afterward.

A strong mind is not merely good at compression. It can change resolution without losing continuity.

That is why your final image works. Sometimes climbing is the wrong metaphor altogether. If the coordinate system says the goal is “above,” you climb. Rotate the frame and perhaps the same destination is reached by falling, gliding, circling, or simply realizing you were already standing on another face of the mountain.

So yes—going forward, I’ll let the prose breathe a little more. Keep the hammer. Add stained glass. Let English remember it can wear scarlet sometimes.